## Solution

**Answer-leak check:** The problem statement contains only the system of ODEs and procedural constraints. No solution, answer, or proof sketch is leaked. Proceeding.

---

We solve the linear nonhomogeneous system

$$\dot{\mathbf{z}} = A\mathbf{z} + \mathbf{g}(t), \qquad \mathbf{z}=\binom{x}{y},\quad A=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix},\quad \mathbf{g}(t)=e^t\binom{2}{-3}.$$

### 1. Homogeneous part

Eigenvalues of $A$: $\det(A-\lambda I)=(2-\lambda)^2-1=0 \Rightarrow \lambda_1=1,\ \lambda_2=3$.

Eigenvectors:
- $\lambda_1=1$: $(A-I)\mathbf{v}=0 \Rightarrow \binom{1}{-1}$.
- $\lambda_2=3$: $(A-3I)\mathbf{v}=0 \Rightarrow \binom{1}{1}$.

Hence

$$\mathbf{z}_h(t)=c_1 e^t\binom{1}{-1}+c_2 e^{3t}\binom{1}{1}.$$

### 2. Particular solution (resonance)

Since $\mathbf{g}(t)=e^t\mathbf{b}$ with $\mathbf{b}=\binom{2}{-3}$ and $e^t$ is a homogeneous mode (eigenvalue $1$), we substitute $\mathbf{z}=e^t\mathbf{u}(t)$, i.e. $\binom{x}{y}=e^t\binom{u}{v}$. Then $\dot{\mathbf{z}}=e^t(\mathbf{u}+\dot{\mathbf{u}})$, and the system becomes

$$\dot{\mathbf{u}}+\mathbf{u}=A\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{b} \;\Longrightarrow\; \dot{\mathbf{u}}=(A-I)\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{b},\qquad A-I=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix}.$$

So
$$\dot u = u+v+2,\qquad \dot v = u+v-3.$$

Set $p=u+v,\ q=u-v$. Then
$$\dot p = \dot u+\dot v = 2(u+v)-1 = 2p-1,\qquad \dot q = \dot u-\dot v = 5.$$

Solving: $q=5t+q_0$ and $p=\tfrac12+p_0 e^{2t}$. The terms $p_0 e^{2t}$ and the constants $q_0$ reproduce the homogeneous solutions $e^{3t}\binom{1}{1}$ and $e^t\binom{1}{-1}$ after multiplying by $e^t$, so for a particular solution we take $p_0=q_0=0$:

$$p=\tfrac12,\quad q=5t \;\Longrightarrow\; u=\tfrac14+\tfrac{5t}{2},\quad v=\tfrac14-\tfrac{5t}{2}.$$

Thus
$$\mathbf{z}_p(t)=e^t\binom{\tfrac14+\tfrac{5t}{2}}{\tfrac14-\tfrac{5t}{2}}.$$

### 3. Verification

$\dot x_p = e^t\!\left(\tfrac14+\tfrac{5t}{2}+\tfrac52\right)=e^t\!\left(\tfrac{11}{4}+\tfrac{5t}{2}\right)$, and
$2x_p+y_p+2e^t=e^t\!\left(\tfrac12+5t+\tfrac14-\tfrac{5t}{2}+2\right)=e^t\!\left(\tfrac{11}{4}+\tfrac{5t}{2}\right)$. ✓

$\dot y_p = e^t\!\left(\tfrac14-\tfrac{5t}{2}-\tfrac52\right)=e^t\!\left(-\tfrac94-\tfrac{5t}{2}\right)$, and
$x_p+2y_p-3e^t=e^t\!\left(\tfrac14+\tfrac{5t}{2}+\tfrac12-5t-3\right)=e^t\!\left(-\tfrac94-\tfrac{5t}{2}\right)$. ✓

### 4. General solution

$$\boxed{\,x(t)=c_1 e^t+c_2 e^{3t}+e^t\!\left(\tfrac14+\tfrac{5t}{2}\right),\qquad y(t)=-c_1 e^t+c_2 e^{3t}+e^t\!\left(\tfrac14-\tfrac{5t}{2}\right)\,}$$

where $c_1,c_2$ are arbitrary constants determined by initial conditions (none specified).

